00:04:01.582 --> 00:04:03.790
So for those of you who are
not familiar with Docker,

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.700
Docker is basically a way
to package your application.

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.610
So it doesn't matter if you're
writing Node code, Python,

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:10.350
Java, whatever.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:12.100
It doesn't matter if
you have ImageMagick,

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:14.920
some random library that some
13-year-old kid in Ukraine

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.540
compiled-- doesn't
matter, you put it

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:19.720
in your Docker container,
and you're good to go.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.630
All you have to do is care
about running the container,

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:26.639
you don't have to care about
running what's inside of it.

00:04:26.639 --> 00:04:28.930
So basically, you take your
code and your dependencies,

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.925
and you put it into some sort
of generic container format.

00:04:31.925 --> 00:04:33.550
And so it doesn't
matter what's inside.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.050
As long as you can
run a container,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.892
you can run all the containers.

00:04:38.892 --> 00:04:40.350
So now you have a
container, you've

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.100
got to actually run it on
your cluster, because logging

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.340
into a single machine, and
then running Docker run,

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.440
and then doing that 1,000 times,
it seems like a waste of time

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:50.940
to me.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.605
So instead, we can use
something like Kubernetes,

00:04:53.605 --> 00:04:56.659
which is a container
orchestration system.

00:04:56.659 --> 00:04:58.450
And what we can do with
that is we can say,

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:01.200
hey Kubernetes, run this
container like five times

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.030
somewhere in my cluster.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.690
So what it'll do is it'll
run your containers for you

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:07.944
automatically.

00:05:07.944 --> 00:05:09.360
And so let's say
you wanted to run

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.110
this container, that
container, that container,

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.276
and that container, and you
want two copies of each.

00:05:13.276 --> 00:05:15.390
You just tell Kubernetes
that, and it figures out

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:16.646
how to run them.

00:05:16.646 --> 00:05:18.270
And so the really
nice thing about this

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.484
is if a server crashes,
it will figure out

00:05:20.484 --> 00:05:22.650
that the server crashed and
run them somewhere else.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.170
If an application crashes,
it'll figure that out.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:27.630
If you want to scale it up,
you want to go from two copies

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.060
to four copies, you can
say, hey, make it four,

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.410
and it'll spin up two more.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.600
You want to go down
to one, because you

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.880
want to save some money,
you can say, hey, make one.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:37.650
It will remove three.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.900
And so this is like
a declarative syntax

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.620
that makes it really easy to
manage these containers running

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:44.370
on your cluster.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:45.850
You might have thousands
of these running.

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:47.760
Trying to do it manually
is going to be impossible.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.635
And how many people have
heard of Kubernetes.

00:05:49.635 --> 00:05:52.640
And keep your hands up
if you've used it before.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.980
About half, OK, cool.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.050
But that's kind of
just a starting point.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.150
Now that you have these
containers running,

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.424
you actually have to
manage the services,

00:06:02.424 --> 00:06:04.965
because you have a container A
talking to container B talking

00:06:04.965 --> 00:06:07.590
to container C talking
to container D,

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:09.799
how do you manage
that set of points

00:06:09.799 --> 00:06:10.840
going between each other?

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.780
How do you set rules
on who can talk

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:16.140
to who, who can talk to what,
how many tries should you have,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.120
how much network
traffic should you send?

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:20.700
All this kind of stuff
becomes really complicated,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.900
and that's where
Istio comes into play.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:26.400
So Istio is a service mesh,
which at the end of the day,

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.420
it means that it manages your
services and the connections

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:33.150
that they make
between themselves.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.390
So if you look at it,
we take orchestration,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:41.010
and we go to management
and communication.

00:06:41.010 --> 00:06:42.694
So you want to manage
how these services

00:06:42.694 --> 00:06:44.360
interact with each
other, because that's

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:47.794
what really causes
microservices to be so useful,

00:06:47.794 --> 00:06:49.460
is the communication
between each other.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:51.237
You can reuse one
service, it can

00:06:51.237 --> 00:06:53.570
talk to three different ones,
give the same information,

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:55.200
you can mix and
match, you can do all

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.050
these really powerful things.

00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:00.470
But it becomes hard to really
understand what's happening,

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:03.560
and Istio makes it a lot easier
to understand what's happening

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:07.480
and control what's happening.

