where you can have
multiple masters,

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.090
so it's highly available.

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.600
So even if a zone goes
down, your cluster

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.360
will still be up and running.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:53.960
You can choose the version
of Kubernetes you want,

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.140
the size of the
cluster, and then

00:11:56.140 --> 00:12:00.496
a bunch of-- not negative
21, let's not do that.

00:12:00.496 --> 00:12:02.120
You can do a lot of
other cool features

00:12:02.120 --> 00:12:04.078
too that Google Kubernetes
Engine gives you out

00:12:04.078 --> 00:12:04.850
of the box--

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:07.730
things like automatically
upgrading your nodes,

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:11.010
automatically repairing them
if they get broken, logging,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:15.482
monitoring, and a lot more
too, for example, auto scaling.

00:12:15.482 --> 00:12:16.940
You can turn auto
scaling on, which

00:12:16.940 --> 00:12:21.530
means that if you have
more containers that

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:24.260
can fit in your cluster, Google
Kubernetes Engine will actually

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:26.780
automatically scale it
up to create that space.

00:12:26.780 --> 00:12:29.704
And even better, if you
have less containers,

00:12:29.704 --> 00:12:31.370
we'll actually scale
it down to save you

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:33.950
money, which is really cool.

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.592
So a lot of that cool stuff--

00:12:35.592 --> 00:12:37.550
and then, all you've got
to do is click Create,

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:39.440
and it'll create that cluster.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:41.773
So I'm going to click cancel,
because I already did one.

00:12:41.773 --> 00:12:45.490
It's like a cooking show, we
have one ready in the oven.

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:46.430
This is still going.

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:51.630
That's OK.

00:12:53.867 --> 00:12:54.825
What we'll do instead--

00:12:57.574 --> 00:12:59.740
because I've already built
this, and I've pushed it.

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:01.610
So we're going to do the
opposite of what I said,

00:13:01.610 --> 00:13:02.943
And we're going to pull it down.

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:24.560
So I kind of cheated,
but that's OK.

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:28.800
So let's run that
container locally.

00:13:32.382 --> 00:13:34.090
So what we're going
to do with Docker is,

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:35.430
we're going to say Docker run.

00:13:35.430 --> 00:13:37.900
And then, we're going to
open up that port 3000.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:39.850
And so if we go
back here, you can

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:42.590
see that it's basically the
exact same thing, except now

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:43.900
it's running in Docker.

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:45.376
And so the really
nice thing is, we

00:13:45.376 --> 00:13:47.000
didn't have the change
any of our code.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:48.500
We just put into a
Docker container,

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:51.280
and we're good to go.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:52.110
OK, cool.

00:13:52.110 --> 00:13:54.440
So the next step is
actually pushing it

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.380
to a Container Registry.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:58.970
And the reason why
we have to do this

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.680
is because we can't
just run containers

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:02.120
running on our local machine.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:03.530
We've got to push them
to a secure location

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:05.180
so that we can run
them on our cluster.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:06.710
And Google Container
Registry is probably

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:08.543
the best place to put
them if you're running

00:14:08.543 --> 00:14:09.970
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:12.000
You've got automatic
authentication and stuff

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.250
and a few other features
we'll look at it in a second.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:18.720
So let's actually push it up.

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:21.570
That's not how you spell make--

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:28.280
M-A-K-E. I love using make,
it makes demos super easy.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.910
So what we do is
run Docker push.

00:14:30.910 --> 00:14:34.391
Can people read that, or should
I make it a little bigger?

00:14:34.391 --> 00:14:35.640
All right, people can read it.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:41.169
So Docker push, and then give
the name of the container.

00:14:41.169 --> 00:14:43.710
So what will happen is, it'll
actually go to Google Container

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:44.210
Registry.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.850
And so here in my
Istio test container,

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:56.990
you can see that we have the
newest one pushed up right now.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.310
And it's tagged as version 1.0.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:02.030
Now, if I enabled it, one really
cool thing that it could do

00:15:02.030 --> 00:15:03.620
is vulnerability scanning.

00:15:03.620 --> 00:15:05.900
So we can actually
scan your containers

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:09.150
for known vulnerabilities
automatically for any Ubuntu-

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.532
or Alpine-based images.

00:15:10.532 --> 00:15:12.240
So that's a really
cool thing, especially

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.450
if you have older containers
that you haven't updated

00:15:14.450 --> 00:15:16.010
in a while, you
go back and look,

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:18.590
and you'll have tons
of vulnerabilities.

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:20.810
So it's a really good
idea to check this out.

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:24.254
It's in beta right now, but
it'll find a bunch of stuff

00:15:24.254 --> 00:15:26.420
wrong with your things and
tell you how to fix them.

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:27.780
And then, you just go--

00:15:27.780 --> 00:15:29.660
usually, you just
update your containers,

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:31.245
and you're good to go.

00:15:31.245 --> 00:15:32.870
So it's a really good
thing, especially

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:36.050
running on production and
running older containers.

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:38.030
So now we have this pushed up.

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:41.030
We actually can start to
deploy to a Kubernetes cluster.

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:45.300
So like I said before, we
already have a cluster created.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:47.760
Let's go back there.

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:50.220
Now, basically to connect to
it, we just run this command.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:54.080
All right, let's run it.

00:16:02.189 --> 00:16:07.610
Great, so now, we can say
kubectl, which is a Kubernetes

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:11.076
command line tool,
get nodes just to make

00:16:11.076 --> 00:16:12.200
sure everything is working.

00:16:19.884 --> 00:16:20.800
Is everything working?

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:22.730
That's a good question.

00:16:22.730 --> 00:16:23.770
Yes, there it is.

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:26.780
So we have a four node
Kubernetes cluster.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:31.012
They're all good to go,
running version 1.9.7.

00:16:31.012 --> 00:16:32.970
So here now that we have
our Kubernetes cluster

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:34.490
and we're
authenticated to it, we

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:38.110
can start running the same
container that we ran locally.

00:16:38.110 --> 00:16:47.380
So what we're going to do is,
do kubectl run demo, and then

00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:50.110
give it the name of that image.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:52.686
And demo is going to be
the name of the deployment

00:16:52.686 --> 00:16:53.810
that we're going to create.

00:16:53.810 --> 00:17:00.200
So if I say kubectl
get deployment,

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.660
and then give that namespace.

00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:03.770
The namespace is just
for this demo's sake.

00:17:03.770 --> 00:17:05.853
Don't worry about it, you
don't need it real life.

00:17:11.839 --> 00:17:16.579
So you can see that we have
our demo deployment created.

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:17.669
And if you say get pods--

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:27.200
and so pods are basically the
containers in Kubernetes--

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:31.070
you can see that we have our
container created, it's ready,

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:32.732
and it's running.

00:17:32.732 --> 00:17:34.190
But now that it's
running, you have

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:36.182
to be able to
actually access it.

00:17:36.182 --> 00:17:38.640
And so this is where it gets
a little bit more complicated.

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:40.520
Where in the old
world, with Docker,

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:42.020
you just kind of
access it directly,

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:43.890
because it's just running
on your local machine.

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:45.264
But in the Kubernetes
world, it's

00:17:45.264 --> 00:17:47.160
running on one of
those four nodes,

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:48.980
and we really don't
care which one.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:50.750
So we want a static
endpoint that

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:54.774
can route traffic
and access that node.

00:17:54.774 --> 00:17:56.690
So what we're going to
do is create a service.

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:08.330
And now I forget what
my make command is.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:13.020
That's OK, I'll just go look,
it's like a cheat sheet.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.890
Let's see-- expose, there it is.

00:18:16.890 --> 00:18:21.450
Cool, and so what we'll do here
is, we'll run kubectl expose,

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.970
and then we'll give it the
target port of 3000, which

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:26.340
is where our node.js
app is listening,

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:30.400
and then run it on port 80,
so access a normal HTTP port.

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:32.140
And we'll open a
type load balancer,

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:36.560
which will give us a public IP
address using a Google Cloud

00:18:36.560 --> 00:18:38.780
load balancer.

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:42.765
So now, we can say get service--

00:18:46.932 --> 00:18:49.390
and I assume most people here
are familiar with Kubernetes.

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:51.431
If you're not familiar,
come and just talk to me.

00:18:51.431 --> 00:18:53.110
I know I'm going
kind of quickly,

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:56.170
but there's more exciting
things to happen soon.

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:57.220
So I want to get to them.

00:18:57.220 --> 00:18:59.710
So you can see the external
IP address is pending,

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:03.520
so let's just run
a watch on that.

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.730
And it's done the moment I
try anything complicated,

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:08.100
it just finishes, it knows.

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:10.760
All right, cool,
let's go to that URL.

00:19:10.760 --> 00:19:12.150
That, not copy it--

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:12.650
all right.

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:23.450
All right, we'll just do this.

00:19:23.450 --> 00:19:24.000
Cool.

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:27.840
And if you go here, you
can see the exact same app

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.300
running on our cluster.

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:33.570
So now, we actually have it
running on a public IP address.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:37.520
If you go there on your phone
or laptop, it will work.

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:39.390
And what we can do--

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:41.900
let's say all of you go on
your phone to this IP address,

00:19:41.900 --> 00:19:46.350
it will probably
overwhelm this app.

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.890
So in Kubernetes, we can
actually just scale it out

00:19:48.890 --> 00:19:51.052
with the scale command.

00:19:51.052 --> 00:19:51.760
So let's do that.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:59.130
So let's say kubectl
scale deployment demo--

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:03.540
the name of deployment-- then,
I'm going to say replicas

00:20:03.540 --> 00:20:08.440
equals 5, and again,
that namespace.

00:20:12.700 --> 00:20:13.210
Awesome.

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:17.500
So it will say
deployment demo scaled.

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:21.652
So now, if we go
back to our get pods,

00:20:21.652 --> 00:20:23.110
you'll notice that
we actually have

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:27.220
five of them running in our
cluster, which is really cool.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:30.690
And then, if you
say get deployments,

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:34.540
you can see it also says that
we have 5 desired, 5 current, 5

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:36.610
up to date, and 5 available.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:39.300
So now, if we go back to this
IP address, it still works.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:41.340
The only difference is
it's now round robining

00:20:41.340 --> 00:20:45.240
traffic between all five
instances of our application.

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:48.960
So you have a lot more
scalability, a lot more

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:49.717
robustness.

00:20:49.717 --> 00:20:52.050
And you might notice that
we're running five copies even

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:54.060
though we only have four VMs.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:58.852
That's because we're able to run
multiple copies on a single VM,

00:20:58.852 --> 00:21:00.810
so we get more utilization
out of our machines.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:06.980
Let's see-- clean.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:10.770
So let's clean that up, we
no longer need that demo.

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:13.390
Let's start looking at Istio.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:17.990
So now we had a single service
running, kind of useless

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:19.370
in the long scheme of things.

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:20.510
No one just runs
one thing, you don't

00:21:20.510 --> 00:21:21.810
need Kubernetes to do that.

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:24.296
What you really want to do
is run multiple applications

00:21:24.296 --> 00:21:26.420
together and make sure they
all work together well.

00:21:33.282 --> 00:21:37.710
What we're going to do is
run this same application

00:21:37.710 --> 00:21:40.660
three times and then
chain it together.

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.400
So you might have
noticed in our code,

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.530
I'm going to go back
to it for a second,

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:46.650
that basically I
can set whatever

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:50.900
I want as the upstream URI
using an environment variable.

00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:53.430
And so what I can do is
actually tie multiple

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:55.890
of these together using
one to talk to the other

00:21:55.890 --> 00:21:59.040
to talk to the other, and then
finally to time.jsontest.com.

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:01.770
And then, they will all
concatenate their responses,

00:22:01.770 --> 00:22:05.310
and finally show
it to the end user.

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:06.660
So that's a really cool thing.

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:09.000
In Kubernetes, we can
actually dynamically set

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:11.730
these environment variables
when we're deploying.

00:22:11.730 --> 00:22:14.532
So let's actually look
at that and so normally

00:22:14.532 --> 00:22:15.990
in Kubernetes, what
we would do is,

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:17.650
we would create a YAML file.

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:20.560
And I'll make this
a little bigger.

00:22:20.560 --> 00:22:24.070
And in this YAML file, we
will define our deployments.

00:22:24.070 --> 00:22:26.380
So I'm going to make
four deployments.

00:22:26.380 --> 00:22:29.990
So the first one is going to
be called front end prod, so

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:31.199
front end production service.

00:22:31.199 --> 00:22:33.739
As you can see here, I'm giving
it the service name front end

00:22:33.739 --> 00:22:34.300
prod.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:38.110
And the upstream URI, I'm
going to call it middleware.

00:22:38.110 --> 00:22:40.240
And so Kubernetes
will automatically

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:42.580
do DNS-based service discovery.

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:45.310
So I can actually find
my middleware application

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:47.424
by just pointing
it at middleware,

00:22:47.424 --> 00:22:48.340
which was really cool.

00:22:48.340 --> 00:22:50.879
I don't need IP addresses
or anything like that.

00:22:50.879 --> 00:22:52.420
And you can see
here, we're giving it

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:56.230
some labels, app front
end, version prod, and then

00:22:56.230 --> 00:22:59.595
the same container name
that we used before.

00:22:59.595 --> 00:23:01.220
And then, in our
middleware, everything

00:23:01.220 --> 00:23:04.600
looks exactly the same, except
it's app middleware version

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:05.356
prod.

00:23:05.356 --> 00:23:06.980
And then the service
name is different,

00:23:06.980 --> 00:23:10.010
and the upstream
URI is now back end.

00:23:10.010 --> 00:23:13.020
Then, we also have a Canary
version of our middleware.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.417
So it's like our test
version that we're just

00:23:15.417 --> 00:23:17.000
kind of working on,
but we want to see

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.370
how it works in the real world.

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:20.969
So again, this also
points back end.

00:23:20.969 --> 00:23:22.510
And then, finally
our back end, which

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:26.590
points to time.jsontest.com.

00:23:26.590 --> 00:23:31.090
So at this point, we can
deploy it into our cluster.

00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:42.330
And so now, we'll create
these deployments.

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:43.800
But of course, we'll
also need to create

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.780
the corresponding services
so they can actually

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:47.110
find each other.

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:49.570
So let's take a look at that.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:52.480
So here in our services.YAML,
it's pretty straightforward.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:54.750
We have a front end, a
middleware, and a back end

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:56.610
service.

00:23:56.610 --> 00:24:01.260
They all open up port 3000 to
port 80, which makes sense.

00:24:01.260 --> 00:24:03.630
The only difference here
is the front end service

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:09.030
is of type load balancer so that
we get that public IP address.

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:12.310
So let's go to that public IP
address and see what happens.

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.446
Great, OK, still
pending, that's fine.

00:24:24.446 --> 00:24:27.701
Let's do it one more time.

00:24:27.701 --> 00:24:28.200
There we go.

00:24:33.130 --> 00:24:37.740
OK so interesting,
it was a little bit

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:39.210
different than before.

00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:42.482
You can see that our front end
prod went to our middleware,

00:24:42.482 --> 00:24:44.190
then the middleware
went to our back end,

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:47.730
and our back end went
to time.jsontest.com.

00:24:47.730 --> 00:24:51.150
But we're getting a
404, which is really

00:24:51.150 --> 00:24:54.060
weird, because I mean,
if I go to this website,

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:54.990
clearly it works.

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:57.840
We were seeing it working
all this time before, right?

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:02.250
It's working, so why
are we getting a 404?

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:05.340
And so now, we go into
the world of Istio.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:09.580
So I actually have Istio
already running in this cluster.

00:25:09.580 --> 00:25:11.530
And what it's just
going to do is,

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:13.480
it's going to really
lock down and help

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:15.640
you manage your microservices.

00:25:15.640 --> 00:25:18.340
So it knows that
time.jsontest.com

00:25:18.340 --> 00:25:20.230
is an external service.

00:25:20.230 --> 00:25:23.470
And by default, it's going to
block all traffic going out

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:24.380
of your cluster.

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:25.970
And this is really good
for security reasons,

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:28.303
you don't want your app just
talking to random endpoints

00:25:28.303 --> 00:25:29.650
on the internet.

00:25:29.650 --> 00:25:31.750
You want you as a
cluster administrator

00:25:31.750 --> 00:25:35.560
to be able to lock down and
only talk to trusted endpoints.

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:37.210
So by default,
everything is blocked

00:25:37.210 --> 00:25:39.460
that's going out
of your cluster.

00:25:39.460 --> 00:25:40.690
So how do you unblock it?

00:25:40.690 --> 00:25:44.590
Well, in Istio, we have
something called egress rules.

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:46.210
So it's a pretty simple thing.

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:49.330
You can see it's only
like a few lines of YAML.

00:25:49.330 --> 00:25:51.790
And so here, we're going
to say allow traffic

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:57.220
to time.jsontest.com on
both port 80 for HTP and 443

00:25:57.220 --> 00:25:59.320
for HTPS.

00:25:59.320 --> 00:26:00.510
So let's deploy that rule.

00:26:09.060 --> 00:26:12.470
And for Istio, we use the Istio
control command line tool.

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:16.675
It's just like the kubctl,
or kubctl, Istioctl,

00:26:16.675 --> 00:26:18.050
I don't know how
to pronounce it,

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:20.420
your guess is as good as mine.

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:24.290
But it's very similar
to the kubctl tool.

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:25.760
And so now, we've created that.

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:30.001
And we can go back to our
web site, and hit refresh,

00:26:30.001 --> 00:26:32.000
and you can see it's all
working perfectly fine.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:36.810
So we've kind of whitelisted
traffic to time.jsontest.com.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:39.420
Now, you might notice
another thing going on.

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:45.780
If I hit refresh, just look
at this line right here.

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:50.280
Sometimes it says canary
and sometimes it says prod.

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:52.560
And So the reason why
this is happening is,

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:54.960
by default, Kubernetes
will use round robin load

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:57.090
balancing for its services.

00:26:57.090 --> 00:26:58.800
So our middleware
service is pointing

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:02.160
to any deployment
or any pod that

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:04.500
has the tag app middleware.

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:07.080
But both the Canary
and the prod versions

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:09.600
both have that tag, so
Kubernetes will blindly

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:11.550
send traffic to both.

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:13.950
In fact, if you had
three versions of Canary

00:27:13.950 --> 00:27:15.930
and only two versions
of your prod,

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:18.330
a disproportionate amount of
traffic would go to canary.

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:20.100
In fact, 3 to 2.

00:27:20.100 --> 00:27:22.080
Because it's just round robin.

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:25.170
So with Istio, we can
actually make traffic

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:27.621
go exactly where we want.

00:27:27.621 --> 00:27:28.620
Let's do that real fast.

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:42.380
So what we can do is set
something called a route rule.

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:45.220
And basically, we
can send whenever

00:27:45.220 --> 00:27:50.270
the destination is middleware,
always send it to prod.

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:52.270
And then, whenever the
destination is front end,

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:55.600
send it to prod, and back into
prod, super simple things.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:58.100
But what this happens
is, now I hit refresh,

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:01.310
it will always go to
our production service.

00:28:01.310 --> 00:28:03.532
So as an end user, I never
have to worry about it.

00:28:03.532 --> 00:28:04.990
I'll always hit
production, I won't

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:07.061
hit some random test
build that's running

00:28:07.061 --> 00:28:08.560
in my cluster, which
is really nice.

00:28:11.540 --> 00:28:12.290
But this is great.

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:15.190
This app is like bulletproof,
so simple it never breaks.

00:28:15.190 --> 00:28:18.786
But in the real world,
our code is not perfect.

00:28:18.786 --> 00:28:21.160
I don't write perfect code,
you don't write perfect code,

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:23.320
let's be honest with each other.

00:28:23.320 --> 00:28:26.270
In the real world, things
break all the time.

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:29.620
So to simulate that, I have
this really cool function

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:31.165
called create issues.

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:35.710
And what create
issues will do is,

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:37.870
it'll look at a
header called fail.

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:41.020
And then, it will generally
generate a number,

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:44.620
and if it's less than that,
it'll just return a 500.

00:28:44.620 --> 00:28:48.940
Yeah, I didn't know how else to
make things break on purpose.

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:51.640
It's hard to make
things break on purpose.

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:53.180
So if you've ever--
this is going

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:54.770
to be a program called Postman.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:56.890
If you've never used it
before, it's awesome.

00:28:56.890 --> 00:29:00.060
Basically, it lets you set
headers in json and things

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:01.240
like that automatically.

00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:04.252
So let's just do a
normal request right now.

00:29:04.252 --> 00:29:06.210
You can see it works just
the same way as doing

00:29:06.210 --> 00:29:08.460
a request from the web browser.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:12.640
But now, we can actually
send headers from this tool.

00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:15.330
And let's set a value of 0.3.

00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.260
So it's a 30% chance of failure.

00:29:17.260 --> 00:29:18.780
Let's see what happens.

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:22.770
Boom, back end failed,
boom, middleware failed.

00:29:22.770 --> 00:29:26.250
Everything worked,
back end failed again,

00:29:26.250 --> 00:29:29.680
everything worked again,
middleware failed,

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.200
there, everything failed.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.450
And we might notice that
the problem is actually

00:29:34.450 --> 00:29:37.810
worse than we think, because
in our code what we're actually

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:41.904
doing is, we're
propagating these headers.

00:29:41.904 --> 00:29:43.570
These are for tracing,
we'll take a look

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:44.720
at that in a second.

00:29:44.720 --> 00:29:46.690
But we're actually
forwarding this fail header

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:48.350
along each request.

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:51.370
So it's not just a 30%
chance, it's a 30% times 30%

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:54.040
times 30% chance of failure.

00:29:54.040 --> 00:29:56.224
And that's where all
these cascading failures

00:29:56.224 --> 00:29:57.640
come into play
with microservices,

00:29:57.640 --> 00:30:02.320
because one failure can trigger
tons of failures downstream.

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:05.400
So let's take a look how
Istio can help us with this.

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:09.980
So let's do our
second routing rules.

00:30:09.980 --> 00:30:13.320
Let's do something called
a simple retry policy.

00:30:13.320 --> 00:30:16.130
So let's have Istio
automatically retry the request

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:18.470
three times before giving up.

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:21.740
And again, we don't have
to change our code at all.

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:25.910
Istio is transparently proxying
all of these network calls--

00:30:25.910 --> 00:30:27.710
proxying is a strong
word-- transparently

00:30:27.710 --> 00:30:30.390
managing all these
network calls.

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:34.720
So what happens is,
our code doesn't

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:37.510
have to know at all that Istio
is trying it three times.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:39.130
It just tries it once.

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:41.179
And Istio actually
manages all the back off,

00:30:41.179 --> 00:30:42.970
and the retries, and
all that kind of stuff

00:30:42.970 --> 00:30:44.420
for you automatically.

00:30:44.420 --> 00:30:45.730
So you don't have to.

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:47.320
So now, if we go
back to Postman,

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:48.610
let's see what happens.

00:30:48.610 --> 00:30:51.550
Boom, working.

00:30:51.550 --> 00:30:55.210
Still some issues,
working, working, working,

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:58.100
working, much better.

00:30:58.100 --> 00:30:59.940
Obviously, it didn't
fix the issue.

00:30:59.940 --> 00:31:05.050
In fact, if I increase this
to something like version 0.5,

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:09.940
you see it's going to
fail a lot more, or not.

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:12.310
It might not fail,
it might mask that.

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:13.280
You know what?

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:17.800
0.9, all right, see, failure.

00:31:17.800 --> 00:31:20.620
And you might notice it's
failing a lot at the front,

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:23.190
too.

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:25.120
So let's take a
look at two things.

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:28.790
One, it's able to mask--

00:31:28.790 --> 00:31:31.890
M-A-S-K-- your failures.

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:35.100
But you don't want
it to always do that.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:38.520
Just because things are working
doesn't mean things are good.

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:42.240
You want to be able to
detect that you have errors,

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:44.810
and be able to detect
that and fix it.

00:31:44.810 --> 00:31:47.460
So Istio actually
gives you a lot

00:31:47.460 --> 00:31:50.364
of tooling out of the box
to manage your systems.

00:31:50.364 --> 00:31:51.780
The first thing I
want to show you

00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:54.360
is something called
service graph.

00:31:54.360 --> 00:31:58.320
So because Istio is sitting and
intercepting all those network

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:01.350
calls, it's actually able
to create a full picture

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:03.000
of your services for you.

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:05.610
So you can see here, we have a
front end talking to our prod

00:32:05.610 --> 00:32:07.110
and canary, talking
to our back end,

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:11.090
just talking to
time.jsontest.com--

00:32:11.090 --> 00:32:17.100
we can also start doing
things, automatically

00:32:17.100 --> 00:32:20.220
start getting metrics
from our cluster as well.

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:23.250
Wow, that looks
way too zoomed in.

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:25.510
All right, let's zoom
out a little bit.

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:28.580
You can see here, once we
started adding those errors,

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.950
our global success rate just
started crashing to the bottom.

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:34.500
And so Istio will actually
automatically find

00:32:34.500 --> 00:32:39.810
your 500s, your 400s, your
volume, your QPS, your latency,

00:32:39.810 --> 00:32:41.730
all this stuff
automatically for you,

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:45.100
and start throwing it onto
Prometheus and other dashboards

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:47.100
so you can start putting
them into your systems,

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:49.530
and start running metrics,
and understanding what

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:51.660
is going on in your services.

00:32:51.660 --> 00:32:53.600
And I didn't have to
write any code for this.

00:32:53.600 --> 00:32:58.440
Istio gives me all these
metrics out of the box for free.

00:32:58.440 --> 00:33:02.190
And then finally, we can
use something like Zipkin,

00:33:02.190 --> 00:33:04.540
or Jaeger to do tracing.

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:08.700
So our stack level trace, a
lot of these tools all work.

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:10.760
So let's find some traces.

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:14.244
So you can see here, we
can see our front end talks

00:33:14.244 --> 00:33:15.910
to our middleware
talks to our back end.

00:33:15.910 --> 00:33:18.409
And we can see that obviously
the back end takes the longest

00:33:18.409 --> 00:33:19.910
amount of time,
because it's talking

00:33:19.910 --> 00:33:22.460
to our external service.

00:33:22.460 --> 00:33:31.600
But even more interesting
than this, you can see here,

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:36.250
it actually has back end
2x, 2x, and middleware 2x.

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:38.890
And I can't find one, that's OK.

00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:40.150
Let's see look back one hour.

00:33:47.890 --> 00:33:48.520
That's OK.

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:50.440
So you can see all the
distributed tracing.

00:33:50.440 --> 00:33:53.110
And to do that, all I had to do
was forward those trace headers

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:54.162
along.

00:33:54.162 --> 00:33:58.550
Let's look at that for one
second, those trace headers.

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.481
So as long as I'm forwarding
those trace headers,

00:34:01.481 --> 00:34:03.986
I get distributed tracing
for free out of the box.

00:34:03.986 --> 00:34:05.360
And actually, I
have another talk

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.090
where I talk about open census,
which can automatically forward

00:34:08.090 --> 00:34:09.360
these for you as well--

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:13.590
so even less work for
you as a developer.

00:34:13.590 --> 00:34:15.909
Now, let's do one final thing.

00:34:15.909 --> 00:34:18.179
So here, you can see
when I hit refresh,

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:20.699
I'm always going
to my prod service.

00:34:20.699 --> 00:34:23.909
But as a tester, I kind
of want to actually use

00:34:23.909 --> 00:34:27.179
a Canary service and see what
happens in this new path.

00:34:27.179 --> 00:34:28.679
And the really nice
thing is that we

00:34:28.679 --> 00:34:32.820
can do in-production
testing using Istio.

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:35.159
Because when we have
thousands of services,

00:34:35.159 --> 00:34:38.070
it's impossible to launch
them all in our local machine

00:34:38.070 --> 00:34:39.570
and test it that way.

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:42.019
We want to kind of
push our service.

00:34:42.019 --> 00:34:43.560
You think it's
working, so we push it

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:46.080
into a production cluster, but
we don't want any production

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:46.920
traffic hitting it.

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:49.050
We just want our test
traffic hitting it.

00:34:49.050 --> 00:34:51.630
So what we're going to do is
put up the last and final route

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:52.130
rule.

00:34:55.152 --> 00:34:56.860
And it's going to make
a new route called

00:34:56.860 --> 00:34:58.540
the middleware canary route.

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:02.560
And what it's going to look for
is a header called x-dev-user.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:05.320
And then, whenever it sees
a value of super secret,

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:07.330
it's going to route
it to our Canary.

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:09.964
So all normal traffic will
go to our production service.

00:35:09.964 --> 00:35:11.380
And then, the super
secret traffic

00:35:11.380 --> 00:35:12.942
will go to our Canary service.

00:35:12.942 --> 00:35:14.650
Obviously, you'd use
something like OAuth

00:35:14.650 --> 00:35:16.510
or some sort of
authentication scheme,

00:35:16.510 --> 00:35:22.190
not just the word super secret,
because security is important.

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:23.940
Let's take a look at
that, so make Canary.

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:30.230
So let's deploy this rule.

00:35:30.230 --> 00:35:32.120
And so now, if we go
here, obviously it

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:35.490
will still go to our
production service.

00:35:35.490 --> 00:35:36.940
But if we go to Postman--

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:39.870
and let's remove
that fail header.

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:42.200
And let's go to x-dev-user.

00:35:51.130 --> 00:35:53.650
Boom, nope, did
I spell it wrong?

00:35:53.650 --> 00:35:55.274
I don't know, let's
see what happened.

00:35:59.370 --> 00:36:01.470
Oh, this is disabled,
ah-ha, there we go.

00:36:01.470 --> 00:36:01.980
Thank you.

00:36:05.072 --> 00:36:07.070
There we go, middleware Canary.

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:11.960
So we're able to route to a
specific service in the middle

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:13.957
of our stack using headers.

00:36:13.957 --> 00:36:16.040
And that's because we're
using header propagation.

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:20.030
So even if you had
like 2,000 services,

00:36:20.030 --> 00:36:25.290
and you want to make the
middle one, the 900th service,

00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:26.630
a special thing.

00:36:26.630 --> 00:36:29.030
By propagating these
headers, we can actually

00:36:29.030 --> 00:36:32.044
use Istio and then
route to that one,

00:36:32.044 --> 00:36:33.710
even if it's in the
middle of our stack.

00:36:33.710 --> 00:36:36.350
So not just the front
end, we can test anything

00:36:36.350 --> 00:36:40.045
in the whole stack using
Istio and header propagation.

00:36:40.045 --> 00:36:42.170
All right, let's switch
back to the slides, please.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:46.930
So we do all this stuff--

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:51.010
thank you all so much.

00:36:51.010 --> 00:36:53.100
If you want to get a
deeper dive into Istio,

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:55.640
it's basically this top with
a little bit more things

00:36:55.640 --> 00:36:57.520
to focus specifically on Istio.

00:36:57.520 --> 00:37:00.960
You can check out my main
dot com slash Istio-101,

00:37:00.960 --> 00:37:02.370
it will have a YouTube video.

00:37:02.370 --> 00:37:05.790
All this code is open
source on my GitHub.

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:09.600
Again, if you go to
that website, Istio-101,

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:11.690
I have a link to my
GitHub repository there.

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:14.970
You can check out Google
Kubernetes Engine at g.co/gke,

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:18.180
istio.io, follow me on Twitter.

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:22.310
If you have any questions,
please follow me out the door.

00:37:22.310 --> 00:37:24.520
I'm going to go staff the
office hours right now,

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:27.150
so come talk to me there,
or talk to me right outside,

00:37:27.150 --> 00:37:28.350
or find me in the sandbox.

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:29.980
I'm happy to answer
any questions.

00:37:29.980 --> 00:37:32.112
I know we went kind
of quickly today.

00:37:32.112 --> 00:37:33.550
But yeah, thank you all so much.

00:37:33.550 --> 00:37:36.230
Enjoy the rest of your IO,
enjoy the concert tonight.

00:37:36.230 --> 00:37:38.330
And I'll see you out there.

